# PSIO type annotations — rules for AI coding agents and reviewers

Terse rules for writing and reviewing PSIO-reflected types. Each rule
links to the section of [annotations.md](annotations.md) that states and
justifies it; the walk-through is [tutorial-annotations.md](tutorial-annotations.md).
Do not invent syntax: every spelling you may use is in the
[master table](annotations.md#4-master-table).

The C++ snippets are consecutive lines of
[`cpp/tests/doc_annotations_examples.cpp`](../cpp/tests/doc_annotations_examples.cpp)
(test `psio_doc_annotations_examples`).

## The model in five sentences

1. One `PSIO_REFLECT` with annotations defines the type in every format:
   pssz, key, JSON / I-JSON, Schema IR, WIT, GraphQL.
2. Bounds are written **on the member**, not by changing its type.
3. The pssz offset width (1, 2 or 4 bytes) is **part of the type**: an
   explicit `maxDynamicData` always rules, else the inferred bound rounded
   up to the next width. Every type with offsets — record, tuple, list,
   map, set, array — has its own width; a record's cap also narrows the
   non-record containers beneath it.
4. A published type changes its layout in exactly one way: `std::optional`
   members appended at the end, at an unchanged width. An existing bound
   may only widen; nothing else about an existing member changes.
5. Untrusted bytes are validated; views and `psio::trusted` check nothing
   you can rely on.

## Decision rules

### Declaring a type

| # | Rule | Why / reference |
|---|---|---|
| D1 | Keep the member types the code wants (`std::string`, `std::vector<T>`, `std::map<K, V>`). **Never change a member's type to express a bound; annotate** with `attr(member, specs)`. Use `psio::bounded<T, N>` / `psio::utf8_string<N>` only where the existing code already uses them, or for a variant alternative whose narrowed content must be read through a view. | [2.3](annotations.md#23-wrapper-types), [5.3](annotations.md#53-element-keys-values-ati--positions-below-the-member) |
| D2 | **If a member is variable-size, give it a bound** (`max<N>`) unless it is genuinely unbounded content (a document body, a blob). One unbounded member makes a type without a cap 32-bit. | [3](annotations.md#3-width-what-a-bound-buys) |
| D3 | Bound **every level**: `max<N> \| element(max<M>)` for a list of strings, `keys(...)` / `values(...)` for a map, `at<I>(...)` for a tuple, pair or variant. A count without element bounds bounds nothing. Each bounded list / map / tuple gets its own narrow offsets. | [5.3](annotations.md#53-element-keys-values-ati--positions-below-the-member) |
| D4 | **Human-readable text is `utf8`; bytes are raw.** `std::string` without `utf8` is raw bytes (Rust `RawString`); with `utf8` it is Rust `String`. Byte data: `std::vector<std::uint8_t>` / `std::array<std::uint8_t, N>`. `utf8` is always enforced; `utf8_spec{}` spelled out is the same thing. | [5.2](annotations.md#52-utf8--text-or-raw-bytes), [6.5](annotations.md#65-bytes-and-hex) |
| D5 | **Set `maxDynamicData(N)` on every type that may gain a field** (rows, action arguments, messages), from its first version. Pick N by [the cap rules below](#how-to-pick-a-cap). | [3.4](annotations.md#34-how-to-pick-a-cap), [5.4](annotations.md#54-maxdynamicdatan) |
| D6 | Use `definitionWillNotChange()` only for values closed forever (point, hash, id pair). It removes the header and all evolution. | [5.5](annotations.md#55-definitionwillnotchange) |
| D7 | Wrap ids, keys and quantities in a `transparent()` type. **A bounded string or list with a name of its own (a memo, a URL, a tag list) is a `transparent()` type over an annotated member**: `PSIO_REFLECT(Memo, transparent(), attr(contents, utf8 \| max<80>))`. It is `psio::utf8_string<80>` on the wire; use it instead of repeating the bound at every member and parameter. The member takes `utf8`, `max<N>` and position specs only. | [5.7](annotations.md#57-transparent) |
| D8 | A rule bounds cannot express is an `invariant(fn)`: `noexcept`, returns `psio::codec_status`, never throws, depends only on the value. | [5.8](annotations.md#58-invariantfn) |
| D9 | Give an enum an explicit underlying type and `PSIO_REFLECT_ENUM`. | [6.3](annotations.md#63-enums) |
| D10 | Annotate integer text form (`json_string`, `ijson_number`, …) only when a client requires one fixed form. JavaScript clients read I-JSON. | [5.9](annotations.md#59-json_number-json_string-ijson_number-ijson_string) |
| D11 | A method's parameters are annotated like members; give `method(...)` a `maxDynamicData(N)` if it may ever gain a parameter; annotate bounded results with `returns(...)` and encode them with `psio::encode_method_result<T, I>`. | [5.11](annotations.md#511-methodname-) |
| D12 | `static_assert` the width (and, when no cap is declared, the inferred bound) next to every published type. | [3](annotations.md#3-width-what-a-bound-buys) |
| D13 | A range is `min<A> \| max<B>` (one spec, `psio::length_bound{.max = B, .min = A}`). `exact<N>` / `min<N>` are for the member itself only. | [5.1](annotations.md#51-maxn-exactn-minn--length-bounds) |
| D14 | **A child record keeps its own width under any cap.** Give every record that is held by a capped record its own cap or member bounds, and `static_assert(!psio::pssz_child_wider_than_cap_v<Parent>)`. | [3.3](annotations.md#33-a-cap-narrows-what-lies-beneath-it) |
| D15 | **A tuple and a method's parameter list have at most 255 elements.** Use a record or a list beyond that. | [6.11](annotations.md#611-tuple-and-pair) |
| D16 | Do not write a member bound to "get a narrow table" when the record has a cap: the cap already narrows lists, maps and tuples beneath it. Write bounds for limits the application has. | [3.3](annotations.md#33-a-cap-narrows-what-lies-beneath-it) |
| D17 | **`maxDynamicData(N)` caps bytes; `maxFields(N)` caps the number of fields** (or parameters), N ≤ 255. Use `maxFields` only for a promise about the field count; it never replaces the byte cap, changes no byte, and cannot be raised or dropped later. A record has at most 255 fields regardless. | [5.6](annotations.md#56-maxfieldsn) |
| D18 | A list whose order or uniqueness matters says so: `sorted`, `unique`, `unique_keys` are enforced by every typed writer and checking reader (order = the elements' key bytes). They are not in the Schema IR: a schema-driven reader does not check them. Prefer `std::set` / `std::map` when the container is one. | [5.13](annotations.md#513-sorted-unique-unique_keys) |
| D19 | Choose bounds you will never need to lower: a published bound may only widen, and a list bound only up to its next width step (255 / 65535 bytes of list). | [8.3](annotations.md#83-annotations-bounds-may-only-widen) |

### Changing a published type

| # | Rule | Why / reference |
|---|---|---|
| E1 | **New fields are `std::optional<T>` and go last.** Trailing `None` is pruned, so old bytes stay valid and new writers that leave the field out write old bytes. | [8.1](annotations.md#81-the-compatible-change) |
| E2 | **A new field must not change the width** — of the record, or of a bounded list / map / tuple that holds the record. Without an explicit cap there is only the rounding slack; if an inferred bound crosses 255 or 65535 the change is incompatible. Reserve with `maxDynamicData` *before* publication. | [8.1](annotations.md#81-the-compatible-change), [8.2](annotations.md#82-what-is-not-compatible-and-how-the-tooling-catches-it) |
| E3 | Never insert, remove, reorder or retype an existing member. | [8.2](annotations.md#82-what-is-not-compatible-and-how-the-tooling-catches-it) |
| E4 | Never move `maxDynamicData` across a width class (255 / 65535) — it changes the record's offsets and those of the containers beneath it — and never lower it; raising it within its class is allowed. Never exceed, raise or drop a `maxFields`. Never add or remove `definitionWillNotChange()` or `transparent()`. | [8.4](annotations.md#84-summary) |
| E5 | **A member bound may only widen** (larger `max`, smaller `min`), and only if no offset width moves; never tighten one. `utf8`, `sorted` / `unique` / `unique_keys` never change. `pssz_evolves_from` checks all of this. Widening is one-way: old readers reject the new larger values, so upgrade readers first. Invariants and enumerator meanings are not checked: treat them as frozen. | [8.3](annotations.md#83-annotations-bounds-may-only-widen) |
| E6 | **Enums: only add enumerators.** Never change the underlying type, renumber or reuse a value. | [6.3](annotations.md#63-enums) |
| E7 | Do not add a variant alternative to a published variant; do not change an array length. | [8.2](annotations.md#82-what-is-not-compatible-and-how-the-tooling-catches-it) |
| E8 | Methods: append `std::optional` parameters only, under the method's cap. | [5.11](annotations.md#511-methodname-) |
| E9 | Prove every revision: `static_assert(psio::pssz_evolves_from<Old, New>)` against a kept copy of the previous declaration. | [8.2](annotations.md#82-what-is-not-compatible-and-how-the-tooling-catches-it) |

### Reading and writing

| # | Rule | Why / reference |
|---|---|---|
| R1 | **Validate untrusted bytes**: action arguments, cross-service data, user input. `psio::from_pssz<T>(bytes)` or `psio::validate_pssz<T>(bytes)` first. | [7.3](annotations.md#73-the-trust-rule) |
| R2 | **`psio::trusted{bytes}` and views only for bytes your own service wrote and stored**, or validated earlier in the same flow. | [7.3](annotations.md#73-the-trust-rule) |
| R3 | A `pssz_view` checks nothing: not structure, not bounds, not invariants. Use `psio::validated_pssz_view` on untrusted bytes. | [7.2](annotations.md#72-what-is-checked-where) |
| R4 | Validate keys from outside with `validate_key` / `from_key`. The key writer checks bounds and `utf8` like the pssz writer: `to_key` throws; use `psio::encode_key_checked` where a status is needed. | [6.19](annotations.md#619-tables-and-keys) |
| R5 | Read views are const: do not try to write through one. In-place writes use `psio::pssz_mutable_view<T>`, fixed-size values only. | [7.4](annotations.md#74-const-safety) |
| R6 | Handle the failure: `to_pssz` / `to_json` / `to_key` throw `psio::codec_exception` (abort without exceptions) for a value outside its bounds; use `encode_pssz_checked` / `encode_json_checked` / `encode_key_checked` / `validate_*` where a status is needed. | [5.1](annotations.md#51-maxn-exactn-minn--length-bounds) |
| R7 | `maxDynamicData` limits the pssz encoding, not JSON text: the JSON codecs do not apply it. Limits that must hold for text input are member bounds. | [5.4](annotations.md#54-maxdynamicdatan) |
| R8 | JSON arguments: write the object keyed by parameter name; accept the positional array too. Binary arguments are always the positional tuple. | [5.11](annotations.md#511-methodname-) |

### Across languages

| # | Rule | Reference |
|---|---|---|
| X1 | Mirror a declaration field by field. C++ `utf8 \| max<N>` on `std::string` = Rust `#[psio(max = N)] String` = TS `field(rawString, { utf8: true, max: N })` = Python `Annotated[str, psio.max(N)]`. C++ `max<N>` on `std::string` = Rust `#[psio(max = N)] RawString` = TS `field(rawString, { max: N })` = Python `Annotated[psio.RawString, psio.max(N)]`. | [4](annotations.md#4-master-table) |
| X2 | Mirror the caps: `maxDynamicData(N)` = `#[pssz(max_dynamic_data = N)]` = `{ maxDynamicData: N }` = `@psio.record(max_dynamic_data=N)`; `maxFields(N)` = `#[psio(max_fields = N)]` = `{ maxFields: N }` = `@psio.record(max_fields=N)`. Mirror `definitionWillNotChange()` = `#[psio(definition_will_not_change)]` = `{ fixed: true }` = `@psio.record(definition_will_not_change=True)`. A missing or different cap changes the bytes: it selects the widths beneath the record too. | [4](annotations.md#4-master-table) |
| X3 | Do not use `exact<N>` / `min<N>` in a type that Rust must mirror (not available there). Before relying on `maxFields`, `sorted` / `unique`, the array form of JSON arguments or a name number in another language, check its cell in the master table: what is marked "not yet available" there is C++-only. | [4](annotations.md#4-master-table), [4.1](annotations.md#41-tooling-per-language) |
| X4 | Confirm agreement by comparing the offset width and the bytes of one sample value in each language. | [Rust, TypeScript and Python snippets](annotations.md#rust-typescript-and-python-snippets) |
| X5 | Use only spellings of the master table. Python: `Annotated[T, psio.max(N)]`, `psio.element(...)`, `psio.keys(...)`, `psio.values(...)`, `psio.at(i, ...)`, `@psio.record(...)`, `@psio.transparent`, `@psio.invariant`, `@psio.enum(...)`, `@psio.interface`, `@psio.method(...)`. | [4](annotations.md#4-master-table) |

## How to pick a cap

In this order ([annotations.md §3.4](annotations.md#34-how-to-pick-a-cap)):

1. **Enforced limits first.** Bound each member with the limit the
   application has (`max<N>`, `element(...)`, `utf8`). Do not replace
   member bounds by a cap: they are separate checks.
2. **Pick the width class.** `N ≤ 255` → 1-byte offsets; `N ≤ 65535` →
   2-byte; larger → 4-byte. Only the class changes bytes.
   - Database rows, table records: **16-bit** (`N ≤ 65535`).
   - Action / method arguments: 16-bit; 255 for a small fixed-purpose
     call.
   - Small values that stay small: 8-bit (`N ≤ 255`).
   - Closed forever: no cap, `definitionWillNotChange()`.
   - Genuinely unbounded content: no cap (32-bit).
3. **Reserve headroom with `maxDynamicData`.** Adding an optional field
   must not change the width. N ≥ the inferred bound of the current fields
   + everything the type may gain. Declare it in the first published
   version; it cannot be added later if that changes a width.
4. **When unsure, go one size up.** Too small is permanent; too large
   costs 1–2 bytes per header, slot and table entry. If nothing argues for
   a specific number, use the top of the class (255, 65535).
5. **Check the children.** Lists, maps, sets, arrays and tuples beneath the
   record are narrowed by the cap; child records are not. Assert
   `!psio::pssz_child_wider_than_cap_v<T>`.
6. **Assert** `psio::auto_pssz_width_v<T>` and `psio::max_dynamic_data_v<T>`
   next to the type, and use the same N in every language.
7. **Bytes or fields?** `maxDynamicData(N)` is the byte cap and selects the
   width. `maxFields(N)` (N ≤ 255) is a cap on the *number* of fields or
   parameters: no byte, no width, enforced at compile time, in the schema
   and by `pssz_evolves_from`; never raised or dropped after publication.
   Most types need only the byte cap.
8. **Bounds may only widen after publication.** Size member bounds for the
   type's lifetime; a list bound can grow only up to the list's next width
   step.
9. **A bounded string with a name is a type.** For a memo, a URL, a label
   used in several places, declare a transparent type with a bounded member
   once (`tut-8c`), and use it as the member / parameter type:

@@FROM struct Memo@@TO static_assert(psio::max_encoded_size<Memo>() == psio::max_encoded_size<psio::utf8_string<80>>());@@

   In a capped argument list (`tut-9c`):

@@SEC tut-9c@@

What fits under 255 (`ref-cap-transfer`): the framing is 23 bytes, the
other 232 are shared by `memo` and `data`.

@@SEC ref-cap-transfer@@

A child record under a cap (`ref-cap-child`):

@@FROM static_assert(psio::auto_pssz_width_v<Item> == 4);@@TO static_assert(psio::pssz_child_wider_than_cap_v<Order>);   // Item cannot use its width here@@

## Checklist: adding a type

1. Write the struct with plain member types. List members in wire order
   in `PSIO_REFLECT`.
2. For each variable-size member (string, list, map, set, nested value,
   and optionals / variants / tuples that contain one): add `max<N>` and
   bounds for every level below it, or record why it is unbounded.
3. Mark text `utf8`.
4. Decide growth: `maxDynamicData(N)` (may grow;
   [how to pick a cap](#how-to-pick-a-cap)), `definitionWillNotChange()`
   (closed forever), or neither (accepts that only the rounding slack is
   available). With a cap: check that no child record is wider than it.
5. Add the assertions (below) for width and bound.
6. Add a round-trip test, one rejection at bound + 1 per bounded member,
   and — if the type has an invariant — one violating value.
7. If the type crosses languages, add the mirrored declaration and compare
   width and sample bytes.
8. If the type is an action argument or arrives from another service, the
   reading code validates.

## Checklist: changing a published type

1. Keep the old declaration (rename it `…V1` in a test).
2. Append the new member as `std::optional<T>`, last, with its bounds.
3. `static_assert(psio::pssz_evolves_from<Old, New>)`.
4. Assert the width did not change.
5. If a bound changed, it widened, and readers are upgraded before
   writers. Confirm by hand that no invariant, enum value or JSON form
   changed (the check does not see those).
6. Test: new-with-`None` bytes equal old bytes; old bytes decode as new;
   new bytes decode as old.

## Checklist: reviewing a diff

- [ ] No member type was changed just to carry a bound.
- [ ] Every new variable-size member is bounded at every level, or
      justified as unbounded.
- [ ] Width is asserted; it is the same as before for a changed type.
- [ ] A new or changed `maxDynamicData` stays in its width class, and no
      child record is wider than the cap
      (`psio::pssz_child_wider_than_cap_v`).
- [ ] No tuple or parameter list exceeds 255 elements.
- [ ] New members of a published type are optional and last.
- [ ] No published bound was tightened (widening only, no width moved);
      no `utf8`, order spec, cap class, `maxFields`, DWNC, enum value,
      variant alternative list or member order changed; no cap was lowered.
- [ ] Untrusted input goes through a validating entry point.
- [ ] No reliance on the RESERVED specs `hex_spec`, `padding`, `skip_spec`,
      `default_value_spec`, `since_spec`, `unstable_spec`, `final_v`,
      `canonical`, `flags` (nothing reads them —
      [5.14](annotations.md#514-reserved-vocabulary)).
- [ ] A `sorted` / `unique` member is not expected to be checked by a
      schema-driven reader (the order specs are not in the Schema IR).
- [ ] Keys are written with `to_key` / `encode_key_checked` and the failure
      is handled.
- [ ] Rust / TypeScript / Python mirrors updated with the same bounds and
      cap.

## Anti-patterns

| Anti-pattern | Do instead |
|---|---|
| `psio::bounded<std::string, 8> tags;` introduced to "add a limit" | `std::vector<std::string> tags;` + `attr(tags, max<8> \| element(utf8 \| max<16>))` |
| `attr(tags, max<8>)` on a `std::vector<std::string>` and calling it bounded | add `element(max<N>)`; assert `max_encoded_size<T>().has_value()` |
| Publishing an uncapped record that will obviously grow | `maxDynamicData(N)` from the first version |
| Adding a required field, or an optional field in the middle | optional, last |
| Tightening a bound in place after publication | never; a bound may only widen (assert `pssz_evolves_from`), else a new optional field or a new type |
| `utf8 \| max<80>` repeated on every `memo` member and parameter | one `transparent()` type `Memo` over `attr(contents, utf8 \| max<80>)` |
| `maxDynamicData(8)` meant as "at most 8 fields" | `maxFields(8)`; `maxDynamicData` counts bytes |
| `attr(f, hex_spec{})` to get hex text or a "strict" check | a byte list (`std::vector<std::uint8_t>`): hex in I-JSON by type; `hex_spec` is reserved |
| `attr(count, max<100>)` on an integer to limit its value | an `invariant(fn)`; `max<N>` is a length bound |
| `psio::pssz_view<T>{untrusted_bytes}` | `psio::validated_pssz_view(...)` or `validate_pssz` first |
| `from_pssz<T>(psio::trusted{action_args})` | `from_pssz<T>(action_args)` |
| A hand-written invariant that checks a list is sorted or duplicate-free | `attr(list, sorted)` / `unique` / `unique_keys`; or `std::set` / `std::map` |
| Catching nothing around `to_key` because "the key encoder does not check" | it checks: handle `codec_exception` or use `encode_key_checked` |
| `std::string` for a hash or signature | `std::array<std::uint8_t, N>` / `std::vector<std::uint8_t>` + `max<N>` |
| An invariant that throws or calls a throwing parser | catch inside; return `psio::codec_fail(...)` |
| Renumbering an enum, changing its underlying type | append enumerators only |
| A different `maxDynamicData` per language | the same N everywhere |
| `maxDynamicData(255)` on a row "to save bytes" when the row may grow | a 16-bit cap (≤ 65535); when unsure, one size up |
| `attr(tags, max<1000> \| element(max<1000>))` added under `maxDynamicData(255)` only to narrow the table | nothing: the cap already narrows it; write real limits |
| An unbounded record as a member or list element of a record capped at 255 | cap or bound the child; assert `!psio::pssz_child_wider_than_cap_v<Parent>` |
| `std::tuple` of 300 elements, a method with 300 parameters | a record, a list, or nested tuples (limit: 255) |
| Assuming a list's offset table is 4 bytes wide when computing a size by hand | the list has its own width: 1, 2 or 4 by its bound and the cap above it |

## Snippets to paste

Width and bound of a type without a cap (`tut-2b`):

@@FROM static_assert(psio::max_encoded_size<Account>() == 3 + 8 + 32 + (8 + 8 * 16) + 8);@@TO static_assert(psio::auto_pssz_width_v<Account> == 1);@@

Width of a capped type (`tut-3`):

@@FROM static_assert(psio::max_dynamic_data_v<AccountV1> == 1024);@@TO static_assert(psio::auto_pssz_width_v<AccountV1> == 2);@@

"Still unbounded" is a fact worth asserting too (`tut-2c`):

@@FROM static_assert(!psio::max_encoded_size<AccountCountOnly>().has_value());@@TO static_assert(psio::auto_pssz_width_v<AccountCountOnly> == 4);@@

Compatibility of a revision (`tut-4a`):

@@FROM static_assert(psio::auto_pssz_width_v<AccountV2> == 2);@@TO static_assert(psio::pssz_evolves_from<AccountV1, AccountV2>);@@

Method arguments (`tut-9a`, `tut-9b`):

@@FROM using OpenArgs     = psio::reflect<Bank>::method_arg_tuple<0>;@@TO static_assert(psio::auto_pssz_width_v<TransferArgs> == 2);@@

@@FROM using TransferArgsV2 = psio::reflect<BankV2>::method_arg_tuple<0>;@@TO static_assert(psio::pssz_evolves_from<TransferArgs, TransferArgsV2>);@@

Round trip and rejection at bound + 1 (`tut-2-run`; Catch2):

@@RUN tut-2-run@@

Both directions of an evolution (`tut-4-run`):

@@RUN tut-4-run@@

Validated read, view, trusted read (`tut-11-run`):

@@RUN tut-11-run@@

How the inferred bound adds up, for writing the expected number in an
assertion ([annotations.md §3.1](annotations.md#31-how-the-inferred-bound-is-counted)).
Count bottom-up; each type with offsets is `k` words and `r` other bytes,
and is `k + r` if that is ≤ 255, else `2k + r` if that is ≤ 65535, else
`4k + r`:

- extensible record, tuple, pair: `k` = 1 (header) + the number of
  variable-size members; `r` = the sum of the members;
- DWNC record: `k` = the number of variable-size members;
- list or array of N variable-size elements: `k` = N; `r` = N × the element
  bound; a list of fixed-size elements is N × size, no `k`;
- a fixed-size member is its size; a bounded string or byte list is its N;
- an optional adds 1; a variant is 1 + its largest alternative;
- a member whose type has its own cap contributes the cap;
- `bitvector<N>` is ⌈N/8⌉ (fixed), `bitlist<N>` is ⌈N/8⌉ + 1.

`Account`: `tags` = 8 + 8 × 16 = 136; record = 3 + (8 + 32 + 136 + 8) = 187.

## Limits to state honestly

- Views are not validated on construction.
- `pssz_evolves_from` compares fields, widths, bounds, `utf8`, order specs
  and caps; it does not compare enumerators or invariants. It is
  one-directional for bounds and caps.
- `sorted` / `unique` / `unique_keys` are enforced by the typed codecs
  (C++, Python) and are **not in the Schema IR**; Rust and TypeScript do
  not have them yet.
- `maxFields` is enforced at compile time, in the schema-driven codecs and
  by `pssz_evolves_from`; it changes no byte.
- The remaining unused specs (`hex_spec`, `padding`, `skip_spec`, …) are
  RESERVED: nothing reads them.
- `maxDynamicData` is not applied by the JSON codecs: a text can hold a
  value that has no pssz encoding.
- A variant member whose `at<I>(...)` annotation narrows an offset width
  inside an alternative cannot be read through a `pssz_view`. Every other
  annotated or capped member can.
- A member with `element` / `keys` / `values` / `at` annotations cannot be
  written through a mutable view.
- A bound larger than the enclosing cap is accepted silently.
- `psio::pssz_child_wider_than_cap_v` is a query; nothing fails by itself
  when a child record is wider than its parent's cap.
- Rust has no `exact` / `min`; Rust's `pssz_evolves_from` refuses every
  change of a bound; TypeScript has no `pssz_evolves_from` (Python:
  `psio.evolves_from`);
  TypeScript has no return-value annotation. Everything else marked "not
  yet available" in the [master table](annotations.md#4-master-table) and
  in [4.1](annotations.md#41-tooling-per-language) is C++-only for now.
- The Rust, TypeScript and Python snippets of the reference are not
  compile-checked from the documents.
